<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dordogne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dordogne is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, in the region of Nouvelle-Aquitaine, roughly corresponding to the old province of Périgord, with its prefecture at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perigueux">Périgueux</a>. It takes its name from the Dordogne river, which crosses it from east to west. In antiquity it was the territory of the Gaulish Petrocorii, and its valleys hold some of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s best-known prehistoric sites, including the cave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lascaux">Lascaux</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dordogne.jpg" alt="Dordogne" width="320" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Père Igor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P%C3%A9rigueux_pr%C3%A9fecture_(2).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dordogne is a large rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> in the Nouvelle-Aquitaine region, roughly half-way between the Loire Valley and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>. It is named after the river Dordogne and corresponds roughly to the ancient county of Périgord. It is the third largest department of metropolitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the largest landlocked one. Four Périgords are distinguished: Périgord Vert, around Nontron; Périgord Blanc, around the prefecture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perigueux">Périgueux</a>; Périgord Pourpre, around Bergerac, a wine region; and Périgord Noir, around Sarlat, which overlooks the valleys of the Vézère and the Dordogne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The county of Périgord dates back to the time when ancient Celtic Gaulish tribes lived in the area. The name is explained by the Gaulish word Petrocore, meaning four tribes. The Petrocores took part in the resistance against the Roman occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. Gallo-Roman remains include the ruined tower and arena at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perigueux">Périgueux</a>, formerly Vesone, and villa remains at Montcaret. After the province of Guienne passed to the English Crown following the remarriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a> in 1152, Périgord came under English suzerainty, and it oscillated between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> for more than three hundred years until the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> in 1453.</p><p>In the late 15th and early 16th centuries the nobility erected most of more than 1,200 châteaux, manors and country houses. In the second half of the 16th century the violence of the Wars of Religion reached a peak partly focused on Périgord, and Bergerac was one of the most powerful Huguenot strongholds. Dordogne is one of the original 83 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> created on 4 March 1790 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, formed from the former province of Périgord.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The prefecture is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perigueux">Périgueux</a>. The population peaked at 505,789 in 1851 and declined to 373,000 by 1975, before rising again to about 417,000 by 2023. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> has more than 1,500 castles, among them Beynac, Biron and Hautefort, along with bastides and abbeys. Writers associated with the region include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-de-montaigne">Michel de Montaigne</a> and Étienne de La Boétie. The caves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lascaux">Lascaux</a> are closed to the public, and a replica, Lascaux II, is open to visitors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dordogne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lascaux lies near the village of Montignac in the department of <b>Dordogne</b> in southwestern France, in the drainage basin of the Vézère.</blockquote><a href="/work/lascaux">Lascaux · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Orélie-Antoine de Tounens, a lawyer from the <b>Dordogne</b> in France, travelled to Chile in 1858 and went to Araucanía, the independent Mapuche lands south of the Biobío.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-araucania-and-patagonia">Kingdom of Araucanía and Patagonia</a></li><li><blockquote>The Petrocorii lived in the valleys of the Isle and the <b>Dordogne</b>, in the modern Dordogne.</blockquote><a href="/state/petrocorii">Petrocorii</a></li></ul></section>
